@page "/dashboard"
@inject AppState AppState

<PageTitle>Dashboard · BevTools</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <p class="bb-overline">Scenario · Rendering</p>
    <h1>Dashboard</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        A realistic component hierarchy: stat cards under a cascading value, a live clock, a user profile and the
        recent orders loaded over HTTP. It is where the component tree, the cause of every render and the most common
        avoidable cost in a Blazor app - renders driven only by a parent - are easiest to see.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> The dashboard</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Plain application code, with one problem left in on purpose: tick <em>clock re-renders whole dashboard</em>
        and the clock's timer calls <code>StateHasChanged</code> on the whole page twice a second.
    </p>

    <div class="bb-demo">
        <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">Live</span><RendererStatus /></div>

        <CascadingValue Value="_settings">
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="() => { _settings.Dense = !_settings.Dense; _settings.Version++; }">Toggle dense (cascading value → re-renders subscribers)</button>
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="ChangeAccent">Change accent</button>
                <button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="AppState.Click">Click counter (AppState) = @AppState.Counters["clicks"]</button>
                <label class="bb-check"><input type="checkbox" @bind="_clockRerendersDashboard" /> clock re-renders whole dashboard (intentional problem)</label>
            </div>

            <div class="demo-tiles">
                <StatsCard Title="Revenue" Value="@_revenue.ToString("C0")" Trend="+4.2%" />
                <StatsCard Title="Orders" Value="@_orders.ToString()" Trend="+12" />
                <StatsCard Title="Visitors" Value="@_visitors.ToString("N0")" Trend="-1.8%" />
                <div class="demo-tile">
                    <h4 class="demo-tile-title">Server time</h4>
                    <LiveClock OnTick="OnClockTick" />
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="demo-tiles demo-tiles-pair">
                <UserProfile />
                <RecentOrders />
            </div>
        </CascadingValue>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Components: the tree and the inspector</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Open BevTools (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd><kbd>Shift</kbd><kbd>D</kbd>) on the <strong>Components</strong> tab.
        <code>Dashboard</code> sits under the site's layouts (<code>Routes</code> → <code>MainLayout</code> →
        <code>DocsLayout</code>) with its children: three <code>StatsCard</code>s, <code>LiveClock</code>,
        <code>UserProfile</code> and <code>RecentOrders</code>. Each row says how many times the component rendered and
        how long its last render took; ⚡ on <code>Routes</code> marks where the interactive render mode starts, with the
        runtime this visit got (<em>Server</em> or <em>WASM</em>).
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Select a <code>StatsCard</code>: its <em>Parameters</em> (<code>Title</code>, <code>Value</code>, <code>Trend</code>), the cascading <code>DashboardSettings</code> it reads, and under <em>Rendering</em> the cause of every render it has had (<em>Why did it render?</em>, then <em>Recent renders</em> with the batch of each).</li>
        <li>The same inspector answers where and when: <em>Type &amp; source</em> links <code>StatsCard.razor</code> (it opens in your editor) and names the render mode it inherits and from whom; <em>Lifecycle</em> says when it was created, initialized and first and last rendered, whether a render is queued, and what its <code>SetParametersAsync</code> costs per type.</li>
        <li>Select <code>UserProfile</code> and expand <code>Session</code> under <em>Injected services</em>: its <code>UserSession</code> shows <code>AccessToken</code> and <code>Nickname</code> redacted - one by its name, the other by <code>[BevToolsSensitive]</code> - and does not list <code>SupportNotes</code> at all, which is marked <code>[BevToolsIgnore]</code>.</li>
        <li>Press <em>Toggle dense</em> or <em>Change accent</em>: the cascading value changes and every card that reads it renders again.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Components in the page: pick, outline, flash</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        The tree and the page point at each other. BevTools follows Blazor's own record of which component rendered
        which part of the page, so nothing has to be added to the markup.
    </p>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li>Press <strong>⌖ Pick</strong> in the panel header (or <code>&gt;pick</code> in <kbd>Ctrl</kbd><kbd>K</kbd>), hover the page - each component is outlined with its name - and click the clock: the inspector opens on <code>LiveClock</code>. <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels.</li>
        <li>Hover a row of the tree and its component is outlined in the page; <em>⌖ Show in page</em> in the inspector scrolls to it first.</li>
        <li>Tick <em>flash renders</em> in the Components toolbar, then tick <em>clock re-renders whole dashboard</em>: every card flashes twice a second. The colour counts the renders since the previous flash: blue once, green 2-3, amber 4-9, red 10 or more.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Profiler and Diagnostics: renders nobody asked for</h3>
    <p class="bb-card-desc">
        Tick the checkbox, then open the <strong>Profiler</strong> and sort by renders: <code>Dashboard</code> and every
        card climb together. Selected in the inspector, the Revenue card says <em>Parent Dashboard re-rendered
        (parameters re-applied)</em> and marks those renders <em>forced</em>, because none of its parameter values
        changed - unlike the Visitors card, whose <code>Value</code> does. <strong>Diagnostics</strong> reports
        <em>Renders driven only by the parent</em> for the cards, the three of them as one finding.
    </p>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <a href="problems#problem-1" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 1, step by step →</a>
        <a href="products" class="btn btn-outline">A bigger cascade: Products →</a>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="bb-card">
    <h3 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> State, Network and Services</h3>
    <ul class="bb-detail-list">
        <li><strong>State</strong> → <code>AppState</code>: every press of <em>Click counter</em> is a named <code>Click</code> change, and every navigation a <code>Visit</code>. Its <code>ApiKey</code> is redacted.</li>
        <li><strong>Network</strong>: <code>GET /api/orders</code>, slow on purpose, and <code>GET /api/profile</code>, whose <code>Authorization</code> header is redacted. No click sent them, so the Trigger column names the component method that did: <em>from RecentOrders.Reload</em>, <em>from UserProfile.OnAfterRenderAsync</em>.</li>
        <li><strong>Services</strong>: every registration with its lifetime, implementation and how many services it depends on and is used by. Search <code>LeakyCache</code> and <code>ReportService</code> and select them: the dependency tree shows a singleton capturing a scoped service and a circular dependency, registered in this app and never resolved. <strong>Diagnostics</strong> lists both.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="bb-try">
        <a href="problems#problem-7" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 7: secrets →</a>
        <a href="problems#problem-8" class="btn btn-outline">Problem 8: DI mistakes →</a>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private readonly DashboardSettings _settings = new();
    private decimal _revenue = 128_430;
    private int _orders = 1_204;
    private int _visitors = 48_213;
    private bool _clockRerendersDashboard;

    private void ChangeAccent()
    {
        _settings.Accent = _settings.Accent == DashboardSettings.DefaultAccent ? DashboardSettings.AlternateAccent : DashboardSettings.DefaultAccent;
        _settings.Version++;
    }

    private void OnClockTick()
    {
        // Intentional problem #1: a timer in a child bubbles up and re-renders the entire dashboard twice a second.
        if (_clockRerendersDashboard)
        {
            _visitors++;
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }
}
